WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.293
Two rules do not automatically connect because they mention the same condition.

2
00:00:05.593 --> 00:00:10.777
A continuing inference needs a result that satisfies another rule’s sufficient side.

3
00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:12.784
Every red badge is numbered.

4
00:00:13.084 --> 00:00:14.748
Every blue badge is numbered.

5
00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:16.541
The arrows meet at numbered.

6
00:00:16.841 --> 00:00:18.953
They do not continue from numbered to blue.

7
00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:23.947
A badge could be red and numbered without being blue, and both rules would remain true.

8
00:00:24.247 --> 00:00:28.513
That is a concrete reason the proposed red-to-blue conclusion fails.

9
00:00:28.813 --> 00:00:31.565
The shared result has not identified the source.

10
00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:35.471
A false connection can produce an impressive-looking long chain.

11
00:00:35.771 --> 00:00:39.995
Test the direction at each join before accepting its final conclusion.

12
00:00:40.295 --> 00:00:44.988
Ask what the earlier rule actually proves and whether that is enough for the next rule.

13
00:00:45.288 --> 00:00:49.619
Two arrows arriving at one result do not give a path through it in reverse.

14
00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:53.332
When you see an apparent connection, read that one join aloud.

15
00:00:53.632 --> 00:00:59.115
A quick counterexample is often more informative than a label such as invalid chain.

16
00:00:59.415 --> 00:01:05.943
Also recognize genuine result-to-sufficient matches rather than rejecting every repeated condition.
